<div class="sheet reader-sheet">
  <header class="masthead">
    <p class="flagline">
      <span class="smallcaps">Privacy</span>
      <span class="smallcaps">No account</span>
    </p>
    <h1 class="masthead__wordmark"><a href="/">The Frontier</a></h1>
    <p class="masthead__motto">Exactly what is kept, in plain words.</p>
  </header>

  <main class="page reader-privacy">
    <h2 class="section-band">
      <span class="smallcaps">What is stored</span>
      <span class="smallcaps section-band__kicker">Three tables</span>
    </h2>

    <p>
      The Frontier has no accounts, no email addresses and no passwords. There is
      no sign-up, and nothing here identifies you to another reader.
    </p>

    <dl class="reader-facts">
      <dt><code>readers</code></dt>
      <dd>
        One row for you: a random ID (22 characters), the first time the ID was
        saved, and the last time it was used. The row exists on your first saved
        chip or thumb, not when you arrive.
      </dd>

      <dt><code>profiles</code></dt>
      <dd>
        Every saved version of your taste document: a version number and JSON
        containing your interest chips, the accounts you trust, an avoid list,
        a map of story links to <em>up</em> or <em>down</em>, and the count of
        each. Older versions are kept when a new one is saved.
      </dd>

      <dt><code>profile_events</code></dt>
      <dd>
        An append-only list of what you did: each chip, trusted account and thumb,
        with its story link, the edition day, and the time. Events are never
        edited.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2 class="section-band">
      <span class="smallcaps">The cookie</span>
      <span class="smallcaps section-band__kicker">One</span>
    </h2>

    <p>
      A single cookie, <code>frontier_reader</code>, holds a signed random reader
      ID. It is HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, and lasts two years. It is not a session
      cookie and it is not used for advertising or analytics. The signature means
      a changed ID is rejected and a new one is minted.
    </p>

    <h2 class="section-band">
      <span class="smallcaps">Your link</span>
      <span class="smallcaps section-band__kicker">A key</span>
    </h2>

    <p>
      The “your paper” link contains a signed token for the same ID. Opening it
      shows a confirmation; restoring it rebinds the cookie. Treat it like a key:
      anyone who has the link can restore that profile in a browser without one,
      and can then read and change it. The link expires after two years, like the
      cookie.
    </p>

    <h2 class="section-band">
      <span class="smallcaps">What is not stored</span>
      <span class="smallcaps section-band__kicker">Nothing else</span>
    </h2>

    <ul class="reader-list">
      <li>No name, email address, phone number or password.</li>
      <li>No IP address, user agent, referrer or location.</li>
      <li>No reading history beyond the chips, accounts and thumbs you save.</li>
      <li>No third-party analytics, advertising or tracking scripts.</li>
      <li>
        Source images load from the publisher's server, so that server sees the
        request; we do not proxy, copy or track them, and we send no referrer.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="section-band">
      <span class="smallcaps">Erase it</span>
      <span class="smallcaps section-band__kicker">Your call</span>
    </h2>

    <p>
      “Forget this reader” on <a href="/your-paper">your paper page</a> deletes the
      reader row, every profile version and every event, and clears the cookie.
      Clearing cookies without it leaves the rows in the database, unreachable
      unless you kept the link.
    </p>
  </main>

  <PaperComponents.colophon />
</div>
